### Why must uploads be processed asynchronously?

Transcoding a video into multiple qualities is CPU-heavy and slow. If done synchronously, the uploader waits minutes and the request times out.

```java
package com.bank.video.upload;

public class VideoUploadService {

    private final ObjectStorageClient storage;
    private final MessageQueue queue;
    private final VideoMetadataRepository metadataRepo;

    public VideoUploadService(ObjectStorageClient storage, MessageQueue queue,
                               VideoMetadataRepository metadataRepo) {
        this.storage = storage;
        this.queue = queue;
        this.metadataRepo = metadataRepo;
    }

    public String uploadVideo(String userId, String filename, byte[] rawContent) {
        // Step 1: Store raw master in object storage
        String rawKey = "raw/" + userId + "/" + filename;
        storage.put(rawKey, rawContent);

        // Step 2: Save metadata with status PROCESSING
        String videoId = generateId();
        metadataRepo.save(videoId, userId, rawKey, "PROCESSING");

        // Step 3: Enqueue processing job
        queue.publish("video-processing", new TranscodeJob(videoId, rawKey));

        // Return immediately: uploader does not wait for transcoding
        return videoId;
    }

    private String generateId() {
        return "VID-" + System.currentTimeMillis();
    }
}
```

### What does the processing pipeline look like?

Raw master goes through FFmpeg to produce multiple renditions, each split into segments, plus thumbnails and an HLS manifest.

```java
package com.bank.video.worker;

public class TranscodeWorker {

    public void process(TranscodeJob job) {
        // Step 1: Download raw from storage
        byte[] raw = storage.get(job.getRawKey());

        // Step 2: Transcode to multiple renditions
        transcode(raw, "240p", job.getVideoId());
        transcode(raw, "480p", job.getVideoId());
        transcode(raw, "720p", job.getVideoId());
        transcode(raw, "1080p", job.getVideoId());

        // Step 3: Generate thumbnails
        generateThumbnails(raw, job.getVideoId());

        // Step 4: Generate HLS manifest listing all renditions
        generateManifest(job.getVideoId());

        // Step 5: Update status to PUBLISHED
        metadataRepo.updateStatus(job.getVideoId(), "PUBLISHED");
    }

    private void transcode(byte[] raw, String quality, String videoId) {
        // FFmpeg: ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf scale=-2:720 -hls_time 4 output_720p.m3u8
        System.out.println("Transcoded " + videoId + " to " + quality);
    }

    private void generateThumbnails(byte[] raw, String videoId) { }
    private void generateManifest(String videoId) { }
}
```

### How does adaptive bitrate streaming work?

The player downloads the manifest, continuously measures bandwidth, and picks the highest quality it can download in time.

```java
package com.bank.video.streaming;

public class AdaptiveBitrateExplanation {
    // HLS manifest (master.m3u8) lists renditions:
    // #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=500000,RESOLUTION=426x240
    // 240p/index.m3u8
    // #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=1500000,RESOLUTION=1280x720
    // 720p/index.m3u8
    // #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=4000000,RESOLUTION=1920x1080
    // 1080p/index.m3u8
    //
    // Player behavior:
    // - Measures available bandwidth: 2 Mbps
    // - Picks 720p (needs 1.5 Mbps) because 1080p (4 Mbps) would buffer
    // - If bandwidth drops to 600 Kbps, switches DOWN to 240p
    // - If bandwidth improves, switches UP to 1080p
    // - Switches happen per segment (4 seconds), no rebuffering
}
```

### Why serve from a CDN edge?

Video is huge and heavily watched. Serving every segment from origin saturates it. A CDN caches segments at edge locations worldwide.

```java
package com.bank.video.delivery;

public class CdnDelivery {
    // Without CDN:
    //   Viewer in Mumbai -> S3 in US-East -> 200ms latency, origin saturated
    //
    // With CDN:
    //   Viewer in Mumbai -> CDN edge in Mumbai -> 5ms latency, origin offloaded
    //   First viewer triggers cache fill from origin
    //   All subsequent viewers get cached copy from edge
    //   Popular videos: 99%+ served from edge
}
```

### Where do you store raw and encoded files?

Object storage (S3/GCS) is cheap, durable, and infinite. The database holds only metadata.

```java
package com.bank.video.model;

public class VideoMetadata {
    private String videoId;
    private String title;
    private String ownerId;
    private String rawStorageKey;      // s3://videos-raw/VID-123/input.mp4
    private String hlsManifestKey;     // s3://videos-encoded/VID-123/master.m3u8
    private String status;             // PROCESSING, PUBLISHED, FAILED
    private long durationSeconds;
    private long viewCount;
    // Database stores pointers, NOT the video bytes
}
```

